<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helsingør</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elsinore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helsingør is a town in north-eastern Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, at the narrowest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. From around 1429 until 1857 Danish kings levied the Sound Toll there on ships passing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg Castle</a>, rebuilt in the 1570s and 1580s, guarded the strait and is the setting of Shakespeare's &quot;Hamlet&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helsingor.jpg" alt="Helsingør" width="320" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karen Rustad · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helsing%C3%B8r_Elsinore_from_sea_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Helsingør stands at the narrowest part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> strait. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, about 4 km across the water, it forms the northern reaches of the Øresund Region, which is centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Malmö. The first part of the name is believed to come from the word hals, meaning neck or narrow strait, and the ending from the Old Norse aurr or eyrr, a gravel or sandy shore. The town is first mentioned as Hælsingør in the Liber Census Daniæ of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar the Victorious</a>, dating from 1231.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1200 the first church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a>'s Church, was built; its oldest parts date from the 13th century and show that the fishing village had grown into a town of importance. The town as it is known today was founded in the 1420s by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, who established the Sound Dues in 1429.</p><p>Ships had to stop at Helsingør to have their cargo taxed, and the income paid for a castle called Krogen, which Frederik II rebuilt in the 1570s as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a>. From 1479 to 1581 the number of ships passing through the strait grew 6.5 times, and in 1672 Helsingør was the third-largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The abolition of the dues in 1857, by the Copenhagen Convention, was a heavy loss, and population grew slowly in the following decades.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town was one of the main transport points in the rescue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s Jews. After Georg Ferdinand Duckwitz, a German diplomatic attaché, passed on word on 28 September 1943 of an order to deport them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> hid Jews in houses, farm lofts and churches in north Zealand and carried them in fishing boats and ferries across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In three nights over 7,200 Jews and 680 non-Jews were taken to safety in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> and Malmö.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Helsingør shipyard, founded in 1882, employed 3,600 people at its height in 1957 and closed in 1983. The Wiibroe brewery, founded in 1840, brewed its last beer there in 1998. The old dockyard now holds a cultural centre, opened in 2010, and the Danish Maritime Museum. Kronborg Castle is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and Shakespeare's Hamlet, set mostly there under the English spelling Elsinore, has been performed in its courtyard every year since 1937. The ferry line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> is the busiest car ferry route in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helsingør” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The crown drew a large income from the Sound Toll, levied at <b>Helsingør</b> on ships passing between the North Sea and the Baltic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Eric of Pomerania introduced the Sound Toll around 1429, a levy on ships passing <b>Helsingør</b> that helped fund the crown until its abolition in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
